    <h1>7 Signs Your Business Needs Process Automation</h1>
    <div class="post-meta"><span><i class="fas fa-user" aria-hidden="true"></i> Facware Team</span><span><i class="fas fa-calendar-alt" aria-hidden="true"></i> November 11, 2026</span><span><i class="fas fa-clock" aria-hidden="true"></i> 6 min read</span></div>
  </div></div>
  <section class="prose-section" aria-label="Article content"><div class="container"><article class="prose">
    <p class="lead">Automation is not about replacing every human decision. It is about removing repeatable handoffs that make good people spend their day moving, checking, and correcting information.</p>
    <h2>1. The same data is entered in more than one system</h2>
    <p>When an order, customer, inventory adjustment, or invoice is copied between systems, the process is slow by design. Start by identifying the source of truth and the handoff that creates the duplicate work.</p>
    <h2>2. Spreadsheets are the bridge between core systems</h2>
    <p>Spreadsheets are useful for analysis, but a recurring export-edit-import routine is usually a sign that systems need a clearer integration or workflow.</p>
    <h2>3. Approvals live in inboxes and chat threads</h2>
    <p>If no one can see who owns the next decision, how long it has waited, or what information is missing, an approval workflow can become a hidden bottleneck.</p>
    <h2>4. Teams reconcile exceptions after the fact</h2>
    <p>Manual reconciliation is often a safety net for disconnected data. A better workflow identifies exceptions as they occur and gives the right person enough context to resolve them.</p>
    <h2>5. Reporting depends on one person</h2>
    <p>When a report exists only because someone knows where to find, clean, and combine the data, the organization has an operational dependency worth documenting and improving.</p>
    <h2>6. Customers or staff repeatedly ask for status</h2>
    <p>Frequent status requests can reveal that information is unavailable, outdated, or trapped in a system other teams cannot access. Visibility is often the first valuable automation outcome.</p>
    <h2>7. The process is understood but not written down</h2>
    <p>Map the workflow before choosing a tool. List the trigger, people involved, systems touched, decisions made, outputs produced, and exceptions handled. That map makes the right first improvement visible.</p>
    <div class="highlight-box"><p><strong>A useful first question:</strong> Which recurring task would free the most attention if it became reliable, visible, and less dependent on manual copying?</p></div>
    <h2>Choose the next step</h2>
